# Runtime dependencies.
#
# This file lists only what the code in src/ actually imports. The previous
# version also carried libp2p, stem, tor-request, aiohttp, aiortc,
# sqlcipher-legacy and orjson, none of which are imported anywhere: the
# corresponding modules (src/p2p, src/tor, src/network, src/storage) are empty
# stubs. Shipping them meant a real dependency on a name that does not provide
# the claimed functionality, and unbounded ">=" ranges that make builds
# non-reproducible.
#
#   sqlcipher-legacy was the sharpest edge: it is not a working SQLCipher
#   binding (pysqlcipher3 is). The correct dependency arrives with the storage
#   implementation, not before it.
#
# Generate a hashed lock file with:
#   pip-compile --generate-hashes --output-file=requirements.lock requirements.in
# and install with:
#   pip install --require-hashes -r requirements.lock

PyNaCl>=1.5,<2

# ML-KEM-768, for the post-quantum prekeys. This is the dependency the previous
# version of this file refused to have: cryptography was test-only, on the
# grounds that it existed to cross-validate our HKDF and X25519 against an
# independent implementation. PQXDH needs ML-KEM at runtime, and neither the
# standard library nor PyNaCl has an ML-KEM.
#
# The trade is deliberate and worth stating: an ML-KEM in PyNaCl would mean
# implementing a lattice KEM here, which is a considerably worse deal than one
# more dependency. The cross-validation use is unchanged, so this is now a
# runtime dependency as well as a test one. The lower bound is 42, where the
# mlkem module appeared; anything older has no ML-KEM at all.
#
# This list must stay identical to [project.dependencies] in pyproject.toml. The
# lock file is compiled from here while pip reads the metadata there, so a change
# to one without the other makes the two install paths disagree.
cryptography>=42,<51
